  "description": "Two fire-rating stories are recorded under the literal comparison: story-1 (\"60 min\" against \"1.5 h\") on a door in wing A, and story-2 (\"90 min\" against \"1.5 h\") on a door elsewhere in the project. A normalization of wing A, effective Friday, names the fixture fire-rating interpretation; story-1's sides still differ under it, so its write records nothing. A retire_interpretation step then removes that interpretation (README section 2), so story-1 is evaluated under an interpretation the reference does not have: it reads open from its recorded period, with its disagreement dimension a limitation at cutoffs where the interpretation is in force and disagreeing before. Saturday's \"90 min\" from A-601 would align it under the interpretation: the write records nothing, and its removal plan is refused with reason interpretation_unavailable. The application, which has the interpretation, writes the alignment end act; it is accepted without comparison with the derivation, while a second end act for the same begin act is rejected. The same kinds of write for story-2, whose interpretation is available, differ from its derivation and are rejected. The transition check lists story-1's acts as unverified.",
  "note": "A normalization naming an interpretation the reference does not have is refused (10.2.9), so the fixture normalizes under a fixture interpretation and then retires it, standing for records made by an implementation that had it (README section 2, `retire_interpretation`). 7.6.14: \"*Recorded results are read as recorded.* ... Its maintenance never revises the unit's transitions on that account (7.6.9) ... Its transition check lists the unit's begin and end acts as unverified (9.5.8).\" and \"*Results it would have to compute are limitations.* ... a conflict story's disagreement dimension ... and the removal impact test for the unit, whose plan is then refused (8.5.2, 10.3).\" 10.2.9 compares an explicit baseline act with the derivation only \"where the unit's interpretation is available (7.6.14)\"; 7.6.5: \"A begin act has at most one end act for each policy\", which 10.2.9 checks whatever the interpretation. The dependents of the refused plan are not pinned.",
